<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Espiritu Santo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Espiritu Santo is the largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, in the south-west Pacific; its main town is Luganville. It was named by the navigator Pedro Fernandes de Queirós, who landed there in 1606 believing he had found the great southern continent. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it became a major American naval and air base, and in 1980 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagriamel">Nagriamel</a> movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-stevens">Jimmy Stevens</a> tried to make it secede on the eve of Vanuatu's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo.jpg" alt="Espiritu Santo" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Bureau of Yards and Docks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Espiritu_Santo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Espiritu Santo, usually called Santo, has an area of 3,955.5 square kilometres, although Efate, where the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-vila">Port Vila</a> stands, has a larger population. It lies in Sanma Province in the region of Melanesia in the Pacific. Luganville, on the south-east coast, is the only true town on the island and the second-largest settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>. Vanuatu's highest peak, the 1,879-metre Mount Tabwemasana, rises in the west-centre of the island, and most of the interior is far from the limited road network. More than 30 local languages are spoken, and the chief system continues strongly in most areas, whatever people's religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Spanish expedition of three ships led by Pedro Fernandes de Queirós landed in 1606 at Big Bay on the north side. Queirós named the land La Austrialia del Espíritu Santo, honouring the Spanish king's descent from the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and believed he had reached the Great Southern Continent. A local chief tried to chase the explorers back to their ships and was killed in an exchange of arrows and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> fire.</p><p>Queirós announced a city, Nova Jerusalem, but in early June, with provisions running low, the ships left. His second-in-command, Luis Váez de Torres, sailed west and found the strait between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> that bears his name. Europeans did not return for 160 years: Louis de Bougainville came in 1768 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> in 1774.</p><p>In the 19th century Australian, British, French and German settlers came to the New Hebrides. Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared the islands neutral in 1878, and in 1906 a joint Condominium replaced their naval commission. During the Second World War American naval and air forces made Santo a large base, with airfields built by the Seabees. The liner SS President Coolidge, converted to a troop ship, hit a sea mine and sank off the island, and the Americans dumped equipment at what is called Million Dollar Point.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Between May and August 1980, during the transfer of the New Hebrides to independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-stevens">Jimmy Stevens</a>'s Nagriamel movement, allied with private French interests and backed by the Phoenix Foundation and American libertarians, declared the island independent. The Republic of Vemerana was proclaimed on 28 May, and Luganville was renamed Allonneaupolis. After independence on 31 July 1980, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>'s army suppressed the rebellion at the new government's request.</p><p>Most people are Christians, with Presbyterian, Roman Catholic and Anglican churches the largest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagriamel">Nagriamel</a> movement has followers at Fanafo. The shipwreck and reefs draw divers. The island is home to endemic birds such as the Santo mountain starling and to two protected areas, the Loru Conservation Area and the Vatthe Conservation Area. James A. Michener set Tales of the South Pacific there in fictionalised form.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Espiritu Santo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War <b>Espiritu Santo</b> became a huge American base, and the John Frum movement spread on Tanna.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-hebrides-condominium">New Hebrides Condominium</a></li><li><blockquote>Before independence, the Nagriamel movement under Jimmy Stevens took over <b>Espiritu Santo</b> and declared the Republic of Vemarana.</blockquote><a href="/state/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a></li></ul></section>
